  <header class="qa-header">
    <p class="qa-pillar">Myths vs Facts · MYTH·12</p>
    <h1>Do Ravens Bring Rain? Raven Weather Omens Examined</h1>
  </header>

  <div class="id-verdict verdict-partly-true" role="note" aria-label="Verdict">
    <strong>Verdict: Partly True</strong> — Ravens do not cause rain. But ravens (like many birds) may alter behaviour in response to pressure changes before storms, giving the appearance of weather prediction. The cultural association has real observational basis.
  </div>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens do not cause rain — that claim is false. However, ravens (like many birds) are sensitive to barometric pressure changes preceding storms and may show altered behaviour before bad weather: calling more, seeking shelter, or foraging more intensively. This behavioural sensitivity may underlie folk traditions associating ravens with incoming rain across many cultures.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Raven Behaviour Before Storms</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Birds can detect barometric pressure changes through the Vitali organ — a structure in the middle ear sensitive to small pressure changes. Research on various bird species shows pre-storm behavioural changes including altered calling rates, earlier roosting, and foraging intensification before weather deterioration. Ravens have not been specifically studied for barometric pressure sensitivity, but the physiological mechanism is present in corvids.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <p>Observers across many centuries would have noticed that raven behaviour appeared to change before storms — calling more loudly, moving in certain directions, gathering in numbers. Interpreted through the lens of folk belief, this became: "ravens predict/bring rain."</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>The Cultural Tradition</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The association of ravens with storm and rain is found in many traditions:<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Norse mythology:</strong> Odin is a sky and storm god as well as a raven god; his ravens circle in storm-laden skies in some kennings.</li>
      <li><strong>Slavic folk belief:</strong> in Russian and Ukrainian folk tradition, ravens calling persistently were taken as a sign of approaching rain.</li>
      <li><strong>British and Irish folk tradition:</strong> "If ravens croak three times, rain will come" appears in various regional weather-lore collections.</li>
      <li><strong>Welsh:</strong> various Welsh weather sayings associate raven behaviour with incoming wind and rain.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>These are folk observations, not controlled studies. Some may reflect real raven behavioural sensitivity; others may be pure symbolism based on the raven's association with darkness and storms in cultural imagination.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>What Ravens Cannot Do</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens have no capacity to cause or summon rain. The claim that ravens "bring" rain is a category error — a cultural belief projecting causation onto correlation. Ravens may respond to weather, but they do not create it. Any pattern observed in raven behaviour before storms is a coincidence of the bird's behavioural response to pressure changes with the meteorological sequence that follows.</p>
  </section>

